  "image_name": "60df6f46465a527c4f06bcf41035428e.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several types of structural disorder in layered alkali-ion transition-metal oxides and groups them by when they occur. The top half shows disorder introduced during synthesis, while the bottom highlights changes triggered later by electrochemical cycling.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes that different atomic interactions drive different defects. During synthesis, local polyhedra can undergo symmetric or asymmetric Jahn–Teller-like distortion, changing the shape around a transition-metal ion. Other panels show cation mixing between alkali and transition-metal sites, disorder within a metal-oxide layer, disorder among alkali/transition-metal sites in the 3D lattice, and interlayer disorder involving misplaced ions or species between adjacent slabs. These examples suggest that steric, electrostatic, and electronic effects all compete to disrupt ideal ordering.\n\nThe electrochemically induced disorders focus on dynamic rearrangements during battery operation. One is transition-metal migration, where metal ions move from their original positions into nearby vacant sites. The other is oxygen evolution, illustrated as oxygen atoms approaching each other and forming an O–O unit before leaving the framework. A legend identifies alkali ions, transition metals, oxygen, substituted anions, crystal water, and vacancies, making clear that the figure is a conceptual map of degradation and disorder mechanisms in these materials.",
